{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["When you acquire a Hebrew slave — the verse speaks of one sold by the court, as it is written: ואם אין לו ונמכר בגניבתו. But one who sells himself due to poverty is written in the section at Mount Sinai: כי ימוך אחיך ונמכר לך וגו׳, and it is written there: עד שנת היובל יעבד עמך, whereas here it says: שש שנים יעבד — for one sold by the court. Some of our masters equate their laws by a gezerah shavah, and if the Jubilee comes before six years he goes free at the Jubilee. And some draw a distinction between them." "And in the seventh — the seventh year of his sale, not the seventh year of the Shemittah." "Shall go out free — a form such that if the lamed had a person after it, meaning he is free, it would have to be pointed with hatef (sheva): לְחָפָשִׁי."], :book :exodus, :voice :rashbam, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {0 "The phrase במכרוהו בית דין appears twice; both instances refer to the court-sold slave, contrasting with the voluntary self-sale treated in Leviticus 25.", 2 "Rashbam's grammatical point concerns the lack of sheva under the lamed in לחפשי; the parenthetical '(בשוא)' clarifies that the hatef in question is a sheva."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:42:24.415168162Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}